# ---- 759 Court Systems and Practices ----
H("759.d.1.A",3,"A student charts how a Texas trial court, a court of appeals and a high court fit together beside the federal district, circuit and Supreme Court layers. This traces the:","structure and function of state and federal court systems",["structure of a school district","structure of a bank","structure of a hospital"],"Both systems have layered courts.")
H("759.d.1.A",4,"The U.S. Supreme Court's main role is to:","hear final appeals and interpret the Constitution",["hold every trial","write all statutes","enforce arrests"],"It is the highest federal court.")
H("759.d.1.B",3,"A chart shows that a speeding ticket in Austin goes to a municipal court while a bank robbery across state lines can go to federal district court. This compares:","the state court system and the federal court system",["two grocery chains","two school districts","two bus routes"],"Each system hears different cases.")
H("759.d.1.B",4,"A case about a violation of a federal statute is typically heard in:","a federal court",["a city park","a county fair","a school board"],"Federal law is enforced in federal court.")
H("759.d.1.C",3,"A crime committed in one county but involving a victim in another raises the question of which court may hear it. This illustrates how:","jurisdiction affects charges and trial procedure",["weather affects trials","music affects trials","fashion affects trials"],"Jurisdiction decides authority.")
H("759.d.1.C",4,"A court's jurisdiction is its:","legal authority to hear a case",["building size","docket color","number of seats"],"Jurisdiction = power to decide.")
H("759.d.1.D",3,"A criminal statute exists to deter harmful acts, punish offenders and protect the public. These are the:","purposes of law regarding criminal acts and behaviors",["rules for board games","rules for recipes","rules for sports uniforms"],"Law aims to protect society.")
H("759.d.1.D",4,"One purpose of criminal law is to:","deter conduct that harms others",["encourage crime","hide offenses","avoid punishment"],"Deterrence is a goal.")
H("759.d.1.E",3,"A student sorts sources: the Fourth Amendment, a Supreme Court decision, a state penal code and an agency regulation. This distinguishes:","constitutional, case, statutory and administrative law",["music genres","car brands","food groups"],"Four sources of law.")
H("759.d.1.E",4,"A law passed by a legislature is called:","statutory law",["case law","administrative law","common sense"],"Statutes come from legislatures.")
H("759.d.1.F",3,"A shoplifting charge for a small item moves differently through the system than an armed robbery. This identifies differences in processing a:","misdemeanor and a felony",["lawsuit and a contract","ticket and a map","permit and a license"],"Felonies are more serious.")
H("759.d.1.F",4,"Compared with misdemeanors, felony cases generally involve:","more serious punishments and more formal procedures",["no courts","no lawyers","less evidence needed"],"Felonies carry greater penalties.")
H("759.d.1.G",3,"A panel of citizens reviews a prosecutor's evidence to decide whether there is enough to indict. This describes the impact of the:","grand jury process",["trial jury verdict","bail hearing","sentencing"],"A grand jury decides on indictment.")
H("759.d.1.G",4,"A grand jury's primary job is to decide whether:","there is probable cause to charge someone",["a defendant is guilty","a sentence is fair","a judge should retire"],"Indictments need probable cause.")
H("759.d.1.H",3,"A student connects the Fourth, Fifth and Sixth Amendments to rights in a trial. This examines the relationship of the:","U.S. Constitution and the Bill of Rights to the court system",["school handbook to lunch menus","map to roads","poem to rhyme"],"The Bill of Rights guides procedure.")
H("759.d.1.H",4,"The first ten amendments to the Constitution are known as the:","Bill of Rights",["Preamble","Articles","Federalist Papers"],"The Bill of Rights.")
H("759.d.1.I",3,"After a major public outcry about a crime, legislators propose tougher sentences. This describes the impact of:","public opinion and the legislature on the court system",["weather on traffic","hobbies on grades","sports on diets"],"Opinion shapes law.")
H("759.d.1.I",4,"Public opinion can influence courts indirectly by:","affecting the laws legislators pass",["choosing verdicts directly","removing judges daily","writing opinions"],"Legislatures respond to voters.")
H("759.d.2.A",3,"In a hearing, an officer testifies, a prosecutor presents the case, a judge rules and a victim describes the harm. This explains the roles of:","police, prosecutor, judge and victim in the court process",["chefs and waiters","pilots and crew","coaches and players"],"Each actor has a defined role.")
H("759.d.2.A",4,"The prosecutor's role in a criminal case is to:","represent the government in presenting the case",["defend the accused","decide the verdict alone","write the law"],"The prosecutor represents the state.")
H("759.d.2.B",3,"During a trial, a bailiff keeps order, a clerk records filings and jurors weigh the facts. This examines the roles of:","courtroom members such as the jury and bailiff",["stage crew","cafeteria staff","lifeguards"],"Courtroom staff keep the process orderly.")
H("759.d.2.B",4,"The jury's main function in a criminal trial is to:","decide the facts and reach a verdict",["interpret the Constitution","write sentences into law","arrest defendants"],"Juries find facts.")
H("759.d.2.C",3,"A victim impact statement and a defendant's testimony both shape how a case proceeds. This analyzes the impact of:","the victim and the defendant on the courtroom process",["the weather on a case","the lighting on a case","the carpet on a case"],"Both parties affect proceedings.")
H("759.d.2.C",4,"A victim impact statement is typically presented:","at sentencing",["before arrest","at the grand jury only","during jury selection"],"It informs the sentencing decision.")
H("759.d.2.D",3,"A busy court moves dozens of plea cases through in one morning, and officials use discretion on what to pursue. This discusses:","assembly line justice and discretion in court processes",["slow justice only","random justice","no justice"],"Heavy caseloads create assembly-line dynamics.")
H("759.d.2.D",4,"Prosecutorial discretion means the prosecutor may:","decide whether and how to bring charges",["pick the judge","write the verdict","hire jurors"],"Prosecutors choose charges.")
H("759.d.3.A",3,"A trainee notes that a witness avoids eye contact, speaks quietly and fidgets. This evaluates:","body language such as gestures and tone",["weather patterns","food flavors","tire wear"],"Nonverbal cues carry meaning.")
H("759.d.3.A",4,"When reading body language, an observer should remember that:","cues are clues and need context",["a gesture always proves guilt","tone never matters","silence means nothing"],"Context matters.")
H("759.d.3.B",3,"A court clerk speaks clearly, listens and confirms details back to a nervous citizen. This demonstrates:","interpersonal communication skills",["silence","interrupting","avoidance"],"Clear communication builds trust.")
H("759.d.3.B",4,"Active listening in the courtroom setting includes:","paraphrasing to confirm understanding",["ignoring speakers","talking over others","checking a phone"],"Confirming prevents misunderstanding.")
H("759.d.3.C",3,"An officer writes a clear, factual note on times, names and actions that later becomes an accurate report. This applies:","writing skills for field notes and report writing",["poetry skills","art skills","dance skills"],"Good notes make good reports.")
H("759.d.3.C",4,"An effective court-related report should be:","clear, factual and organized",["vague","opinionated","disorganized"],"Reports must be reliable.")
H("759.d.4.A",3,"An officer brings a case to a prosecutor, who decides what complaint to file and how to work with the court. This examines the interaction of:","police and prosecutor in filing complaints",["chef and waiter","teacher and student","coach and player"],"Prosecutors review police cases.")
H("759.d.4.A",4,"A prosecutor may decline to file a complaint if:","the evidence is insufficient",["the weather is bad","the officer is tired","it is a holiday"],"Evidence drives charging decisions.")
H("759.d.4.B",3,"Before trial, lawyers exchange evidence and argue to exclude certain items. This explains pretrial proceedings such as:","rules of discovery and challenges to evidence",["cake baking","car racing","chess clubs"],"Discovery shares evidence.")
H("759.d.4.B",4,"The rules of discovery require parties to:","share certain evidence before trial",["hide evidence","destroy evidence","wait until the verdict"],"Discovery promotes fairness.")
H("759.d.4.C",3,"A fingerprint on a weapon is direct proof of contact, while a suspect's distance from a scene suggests but does not prove presence. This distinguishes:","direct and circumstantial evidence",["loud and quiet evidence","new and old evidence","red and blue evidence"],"Circumstantial evidence implies.")
H("759.d.4.C",4,"In a criminal trial the prosecution must prove guilt:","beyond a reasonable doubt",["by a coin toss","by majority opinion","by probability only"],"Burden of proof is high.")
H("759.d.4.D",3,"A defendant agrees to plead guilty to a lesser charge in exchange for a lighter sentence. This explores:","pleas and plea bargaining",["trial by combat","jury picking","parole boards"],"Pleas resolve most cases.")
